# PERSIST-02 (flow B — verify after restart) TEMPLATE. See d3x-core
# docs/nrf/sanity-tests.md §2.14 and §4.7.6.
#
# This is NOT a runnable runner flow on its own — it is rendered by
# scripts/nrf-restart.sh, which substitutes ${SUB_ID} (the
# server-assigned subscriptionId) and ${SUB_VALIDITY} (the
# server-ASSIGNED validityTime), both scraped from
# persistence_sub_create's run, via `envsubst '${SUB_ID},${SUB_VALIDITY}'`
# into a temp templates dir, then runs the rendered flow against the
# restarted NRF. envsubst is scoped to those two tokens only, so no other
# token is touched.
#
# Asserts the subscription created before the restart survived and
# round-trips field-for-field.
#
# WHY validityTime IS A FIXTURE AND NOT A LITERAL
# This file used to assert `validityTime == 2099-01-01T00:00:00Z` — the value
# the create flow REQUESTS. The NRF does not store that: per TS 29.510
# §5.2.2.5.2 it caps validityTime at cfg.MaxSubscriptionValidity (24h) and
# returns the reduced value (tinycore/nrf/handler_sub.go). So the assertion
# had been asserting a value the product never produces, and this pair was the
# one nrf-restart.sh failure (3/4 passing). It rotted unnoticed because
# nrf-restart.sh was not in the standing gate — it is now (xp-168 #2).
#
# The fix is deliberately NOT "assert something looser". The point of
# PERSIST-02 is field-for-field survival, so the harness captures the exact
# value the NRF assigned at create time and asserts THAT value came back after
# the restart. A hard-coded date could only ever rot again; a captured one
# tracks the product by construction.

kind: flow
name: nrf_persistence_sub_verify
description: GET the subscription by its server-assigned ID after restart (PERSIST-02 post-restart).
type: client
protocol: rest
nf: external
initial_state: start
final_states: [done, failed]

states:
  start:
    transitions:
      - event: Start
        target: fetching
        actions:
          - type: send
            message: GetSub
            peer: nrf-peer
            message_body: |
              {
                "method": "GET",
                "url": "/nnrf-nfm/v1/subscriptions/${SUB_ID}"
              }

  fetching:
    transitions:
      - event: rest.GetSub_Answer
        target: done
        actions:
          - type: check
            field: rest.status
            op: equals
            expected: 200
          - type: check
            field: rest.body.subscriptionId
            op: equals
            expected: ${SUB_ID}
          - type: check
            field: rest.body.nfStatusNotificationUri
            op: equals
            expected: http://callback.persist.example.com/notify
          - type: check
            field: rest.body.validityTime
            op: equals
            expected: "${SUB_VALIDITY}"
          # Belt and braces: the captured value must be the CLAMPED one, so a
          # future regression that stopped applying the cap (and therefore
          # persisted the requested 2099 date) cannot pass by making both
          # sides agree on the wrong value.
          - type: check
            field: rest.body.validityTime
            op: not_contains
            expected: "2099"

  done: {}
  failed: {}
